Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 9, verse 88:

When he opened the narrative by praising the God-fearing (al-muttaqun) for their hastening to jihad without seeking permission, he concluded it with that, and mentioned what has been prepared for them. He said, informing (them) of His independence from them, and rebuking and reproaching them by (mentioning) the One who is pure good: ﴿But the Messenger﴾, that is, and He who sent him to turn the servants away from corruption toward rectitude, ﴿and those who believed﴾, that is, with a great faith, being or being those who are (such), ﴿with him﴾, that is, accompanying him in essence and state in all that We sent him with to them, ﴿strove with their wealth and their selves﴾, that is, they expended each of these in His love, ﷺ, and thus they realized the condition of faith. And "lakin" (but) is placed in its proper position between two opposites, because all that had passed of their condition clearly testifies that they had not striven.

Since the context concerns their stinginess with self and wealth, and the deprivation of benefit from their wealth and children, he restricted the praise of His friends to jihad with self and wealth, and did not mention the path. He said "ulaika" (those), indicating that it is coordinated with what is implied, namely: "Those whose hearts I have illuminated, so they understand." His saying ﴿for them﴾, that is, not for others, ﴿are the good things﴾ is an allusion to the possessors of wealth among the hypocrites, because "khayr" (good) is applied to wealth, and its being defined by the article "al" indicates its encompassing all the benefits of both abodes. The expression with the distal demonstrative indicates the exalted station of His friends and the remoteness of attaining it except by a bounty from Him, the Exalted. Likewise, the allusion to them with His saying ﴿and those—they are﴾, that is, exclusively, ﴿the successful﴾, that is, those who attain all their aims, not others;
